Guided Exercise: Monitor an OpenShift Cluster
Objective
Check the health and status of an OpenShift cluster using the web console and terminal commands.
You will learn to:
•	View cluster status from the Overview page
•	Check node services (kubelet and CRI-O)
•	Explore Monitoring and Alert rules
•	View metrics dashboards
•	Filter and inspect cluster events
________________________________________
1. Prepare the Lab Environment
On the workstation machine, run:
lab start intro-monitor
This prepares the cluster for the exercise.
________________________________________
2. Login to OpenShift Cluster (Terminal)
Log in as the developer user:
oc login -u developer -p developer \
https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
________________________________________
3. Get the OpenShift Web Console URL
Run:
oc whoami --show-console
Example output:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
Open this URL in a web browser.
________________________________________
4. Login to the Web Console
Login using:
•	Authentication: Red Hat Identity Management
•	Username: admin
•	Password: redhatocp
________________________________________
5. Check Cluster Overview Page
Navigate to:
Home → Overview
On this page you will see:
Status Section
Shows cluster health and performance.
Cluster Utilization
Graphs for:
•	CPU usage
•	Memory usage
•	Storage usage
Details
Displays information like:
•	Cluster API address
•	Cluster ID
•	OpenShift version
Cluster Inventory
Links to:
•	Nodes
•	Pods
•	StorageClasses
•	PersistentVolumeClaims
Activity
Shows:
•	Running operations
•	Recent cluster events
________________________________________
6. Access Node Terminal
Go to:
Compute → Nodes
Click the node:
master01
Then click the Terminal tab.
________________________________________
7. Enable Host Commands
Run:
chroot /host
This allows you to run host system commands.
________________________________________
8. Check kubelet Service Status
Run:
systemctl status kubelet
This shows the kubelet node agent service status.
Press q to exit.
________________________________________
9. Check CRI-O Container Runtime
Run:
systemctl status crio
This shows the container runtime service used by OpenShift.
Press q to exit.
________________________________________
10. View Alert Rules
Go to:
Observe → Alerting
Open the Alerting Rules tab.
In the search bar type:
storageClasses
Find and open the rule:
MultipleDefaultStorageClasses
Check:
•	Description
•	Expression
This rule alerts when multiple default storage classes exist.
________________________________________
11. View Cluster Metrics
Go to:
Observe → Metrics
Click:
Insert example query
This will show a metrics graph.
Hover over the graph timeline to view detailed metric values.
________________________________________
12. View Cluster Events
Navigate to:
Home → Events
This page shows chronological cluster events.
Example events:
•	Pod creation
•	Container start
•	Resource updates
________________________________________
13. Filter Events
Use filters to narrow results.
Filter by Resource
Select:
Pod
Filter by Type
Select:
Normal
Filter by Message
Enter:
started container
This shows events where containers started.
________________________________________
14. Finish the Lab
Run on the workstation:
lab finish intro-monitor
This cleans up resources and prepares for the next lab.
________________________________________
✅ Skills Learned
•	Monitor cluster health
•	Check node services
•	Use OpenShift monitoring tools
•	Analyze alerts and metrics
•	Filter cluster events

